    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "3F The things authorised by a search warrant—general",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3f"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.43",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting:\n\n(a) to enter the warrant premises and, if the premises are a\nconveyance, to enter the conveyance, wherever it is; and\n\n(b) to search for and record fingerprints found at the premises\nand to take samples of things found at the premises for\nforensic purposes; and\n\n(c) to search the premises for the kinds of evidential material\nspecified in the warrant, and to seize things of that kind\nfound at the premises; and\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting:\n\n(a) to enter the warrant premises and, if the premises are a\nconveyance, to enter the conveyance, wherever it is; and\n\n(b) to search for and record fingerprints found at the premises\nand to take samples of things found at the premises for\nforensic purposes; and\n\n(c) to search the premises for the kinds of evidential material\nspecified in the warrant, and to seize things of that kind\nfound at the premises; and\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1288d9274f6dce1e2cf99171426d99dda44e621da04419fa8a42542366fef991",
      "skos:note": "Restricts entry and search of premises without warrant."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3f",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "3F The things authorised by a search warrant—general",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 43,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting:\n\n(a) to enter the warrant premises and, if the premises are a\nconveyance, to enter the conveyance, wherever it is; and\n\n(b) to search for and record fingerprints found at the premises\nand to take samples of things found at the premises for\nforensic purposes; and\n\n(c) to search the premises for the kinds of evidential material\nspecified in the warrant, and to seize things of that kind\nfound at the premises; and\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f-norm",
      "@type": "values:Prohibition",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3f-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "forbid",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Prohibits actions.",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3fa",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "3FA The things authorised by a search warrant—additional things",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3fa"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.48",
      "values:originalText": "for digital assets\nWarrant in relation to premises—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; and\n\n(b) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects the digital asset to be:\n\n(i) evidential material in relation to an offence to which the\nwarrant relates; or\n\n(ii) evidential material in relation to another offence that is\nan indictable offence; or\n\n\nSearch, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers under\ndelayed notification search warrants)  Part IAA\nSearch warrants  Division 2\n\nSection 3FA\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(iii) evidential material (within the meaning of the Proceeds\nof Crime Act 2002) or tainted property (within the\nmeaning of that Act); and\n\n(c) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects that seizing the digital asset is necessary to prevent\nthe digital asset’s concealment, loss or destruction or its use\nin committing an offence.\nNote:\nFor the purposes of paragraph (a), the digital asset need not be found\nat the premises. Data accessed using powers under this Part may\nsuggest the existence of the digital asset. For example, a thing\nsuggesting the existence of the digital asset could be found by\naccessing data using electronic equipment moved from the premises:\nsee section 3LAA.\nWarrant in relation to person—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(2) A warrant that is in force in relation to a person authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; and\n\n(b) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects the digital asset to be:\n\n(i) evidential material in relation to an offence to which the\nwarrant relates; or\n\n(ii) evidential material in relation to another offence that is\nan indictable offence; or\n\n(iii) evidential material (within the meaning of the Proceeds\nof Crime Act 2002) or tainted property (within the\nmeaning of that Act); and\n\n(c) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects that seizing the digital asset is necessary to prevent\nthe digital asset’s concealment, loss or destruction or its use\nin committing an offence.\nNote:\nFor the purposes of paragraph (a), the digital asset need not be found\nin the person’s possession. Data accessed using powers under this Part\nmay suggest the existence of the digital asset. For example, a thing\nsuggesting the existence of the digital asset could be found by\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)",
      "skos:definition": "for digital assets\nWarrant in relation to premises—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; and\n\n(b) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects the digital asset to be:\n\n(i) evidential material in relation to an offence to which the\nwarrant relates; or\n\n(ii) evidential material in relation to another offence that is\nan indictable offence; or\n\n\nSearch, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers under\ndelayed notification search warrants)  Part IAA\nSearch warrants  Division 2\n\nSection 3FA\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(iii) evidential material (within the meaning of the Proceeds\nof Crime Act 2002) or tainted property (within the\nmeaning of that Act); and\n\n(c) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects that seizing the digital asset is necessary to prevent\nthe digital asset’s concealment, loss or destruction or its use\nin committing an offence.\nNote:\nFor the purposes of paragraph (a), the digital asset need not be found\nat the premises. Data accessed using powers under this Part may\nsuggest the existence of the digital asset. For example, a thing\nsuggesting the existence of the digital asset could be found by\naccessing data using electronic equipment moved from the premises:\nsee section 3LAA.\nWarrant in relation to person—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(2) A warrant that is in force in relation to a person authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; a",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3fa-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3fa-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d468d585384d9b5ba6d082a3583871c87036ba0cd29648c99ac724fb10f468dc"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3fa",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "3FA The things authorised by a search warrant—additional things",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 48,
      "values:originalText": "for digital assets\nWarrant in relation to premises—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(1) A warrant that is in force in relation to premises authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; and\n\n(b) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects the digital asset to be:\n\n(i) evidential material in relation to an offence to which the\nwarrant relates; or\n\n(ii) evidential material in relation to another offence that is\nan indictable offence; or\n\n\nSearch, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers under\ndelayed notification search warrants)  Part IAA\nSearch warrants  Division 2\n\nSection 3FA\n\nCrimes Act 1914\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\n(iii) evidential material (within the meaning of the Proceeds\nof Crime Act 2002) or tainted property (within the\nmeaning of that Act); and\n\n(c) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects that seizing the digital asset is necessary to prevent\nthe digital asset’s concealment, loss or destruction or its use\nin committing an offence.\nNote:\nFor the purposes of paragraph (a), the digital asset need not be found\nat the premises. Data accessed using powers under this Part may\nsuggest the existence of the digital asset. For example, a thing\nsuggesting the existence of the digital asset could be found by\naccessing data using electronic equipment moved from the premises:\nsee section 3LAA.\nWarrant in relation to person—authority to seize digital assets\n\n(2) A warrant that is in force in relation to a person authorises the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting to seize a digital asset if:\n\n(a) in the course of exercising powers under this Part, the\nexecuting officer or a constable assisting finds one or more\nthings that suggest the existence of the digital asset; and\n\n(b) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects the digital asset to be:\n\n(i) evidential material in relation to an offence to which the\nwarrant relates; or\n\n(ii) evidential material in relation to another offence that is\nan indictable offence; or\n\n(iii) evidential material (within the meaning of the Proceeds\nof Crime Act 2002) or tainted property (within the\nmeaning of that Act); and\n\n(c) the executing officer or a constable assisting reasonably\nsuspects that seizing the digital asset is necessary to prevent\nthe digital asset’s concealment, loss or destruction or its use\nin committing an offence.\nNote:\nFor the purposes of paragraph (a), the digital asset need not be found\nin the person’s possession. Data accessed using powers under this Part\nmay suggest the existence of the digital asset. For example, a thing\nsuggesting the existence of the digital asset could be found by\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)"
    },
