    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "3L Use of electronic equipment at premises",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3l"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.63",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The executing officer of a warrant in relation to premises, or a\nconstable assisting, may operate electronic equipment at the\nwarrant premises to access data (including data not held at the\npremises) if he or she suspects on reasonable grounds that the data\nconstitutes evidential material or suggests the existence of a digital\nasset that may be seized under the warrant.\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The executing officer of a warrant in relation to premises, or a\nconstable assisting, may operate electronic equipment at the\nwarrant premises to access data (including data not held at the\npremises) if he or she suspects on reasonable grounds that the data\nconstitutes evidential material or suggests the existence of a digital\nasset that may be seized under the warrant.\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l-logic-epistemic-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l-logic-ltl-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-3l-logic-alleninterval-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "137a3773e7843f747513ba790708aff2454d9b1525886257c3f14f474afce5ed",
      "skos:note": "Executing officer may operate electronic equipment at premises to access data if they suspect on reasonable grounds that the data constitutes evidential material or suggests the existence of a digital asset."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-3l",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "3L Use of electronic equipment at premises",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 63,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The executing officer of a warrant in relation to premises, or a\nconstable assisting, may operate electronic equipment at the\nwarrant premises to access data (including data not held at the\npremises) if he or she suspects on reasonable grounds that the data\nconstitutes evidential material or suggests the existence of a digital\nasset that may be seized under the warrant.\n\n\nPart IAA  Search, information gathering, arrest and related powers (other than powers\nunder delayed notification search warrants)"
    },
