    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-4n",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "4N Children over 10 but under 14",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-4n"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-act-1914.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-act-1914.pdf, p.337",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A child aged 10 years or more but under 14 years old can only be\nliable for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth if the\nchild knows that his or her conduct is wrong.\n\n(2) The question whether a child knows that his or her conduct is\nwrong is one of fact. The burden of proving this is on the\nprosecution.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A child aged 10 years or more but under 14 years old can only be\nliable for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth if the\nchild knows that his or her conduct is wrong.\n\n(2) The question whether a child knows that his or her conduct is\nwrong is one of fact. The burden of proving this is on the\nprosecution.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-4n-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-act-1914-sec-4n-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d42921416fae9cfc25634e6bf8d108e4f2e6aa59098d4cb46207316f2b798c83"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-act-1914#sec-4n",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "4N Children over 10 but under 14",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 337,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A child aged 10 years or more but under 14 years old can only be\nliable for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth if the\nchild knows that his or her conduct is wrong.\n\n(2) The question whether a child knows that his or her conduct is\nwrong is one of fact. The burden of proving this is on the\nprosecution."
    },
